---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Param from "../../../components/Param.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="adjust"
  title="Adjust"
  description="How to set a stacked render's white balance, exposure, tone, color, sharpening and vignette in Deptheos, crop, turn and flip it, and what exports keep."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Adjust</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Adjust sets a render's look: its white balance, exposure, tone, color, sharpening and vignette, and its
      Crop, with quarter-turns and flips. The adjustments are kept beside the render and drawn over it, never
      written into its pixels, so every slider can go back to where it started at any time.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="open">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Opening Adjust</h2>
      <p>
        With a result on screen, click <em>Adjust</em> in the title bar, press <em>A</em>, or choose Stack ▸
        Adjust. The title bar then shows "Adjusting #4" with Undo, Redo, Export and <em>Done</em>, and the
        inspector holds the sliders. Done or Esc leaves Adjust. As with Retouch, a result has to be saved as a
        render first, which happens on its own a moment after a stack finishes. Adjust and Retouch don't open
        together: opening one closes the other.
      </p>
      <p>
        At the top of the inspector, a histogram shows the picture's brightness and its red, green and blue. A
        mark at each end lights when some pixels show at black (shadows clipped) or at white (highlights
        clipped); a single bright pixel is enough. The histogram is of the whole picture under the Crop, so it
        stays the same as you zoom and move around.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="sliders">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The sliders</h2>
      <p>
        The sliders sit under five headings. Each starts at a value that changes nothing, and a render with every
        slider there looks exactly as it did before Adjust. A stack with no color, such as a monochrome one,
        shows no color sliders.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="choose">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Slider</th><th scope="col">Range</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row" colspan="3">White Balance</th></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Temperature</th><td>RAW: <Param path="adjust.temperature_kelvin.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.temperature_kelvin.max" /> K, from as shot. Others: <Param path="adjust.temperature_relative.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.temperature_relative.max" /></td><td>Higher warms the picture toward yellow; lower cools it toward blue.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Tint</th><td>RAW: <Param path="adjust.tint_raw.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.tint_raw.max" />, from as shot. Others: <Param path="adjust.tint_relative.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.tint_relative.max" /></td><td>Higher shifts the picture toward magenta; lower shifts it toward green.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" colspan="3">Tone</th></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Exposure</th><td><Param path="adjust.exposure.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.exposure.max" /> EV</td><td>Higher brightens the whole picture, in stops; bright areas roll off into white rather than clip.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Contrast</th><td><Param path="adjust.contrast.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.contrast.max" /></td><td>Higher darkens the dark tones and brightens the bright ones; lower brings both toward the middle.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Highlights</th><td><Param path="adjust.highlights.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.highlights.max" /></td><td>Lower brings back detail in bright areas; higher brightens them.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Shadows</th><td><Param path="adjust.shadows.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.shadows.max" /></td><td>Higher opens up dark areas; lower deepens them.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Whites</th><td><Param path="adjust.whites.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.whites.max" /></td><td>Higher makes the brightest tones brighter; lower pulls them down from white.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Blacks</th><td><Param path="adjust.blacks.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.blacks.max" /></td><td>Lower makes the darkest tones darker; higher lifts them from black.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" colspan="3">Presence</th></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Clarity</th><td><Param path="adjust.clarity.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.clarity.max" /></td><td>Higher strengthens contrast across broad areas so shapes stand out; lower softens it.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Texture</th><td><Param path="adjust.texture.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.texture.max" /></td><td>Higher brings out fine surface detail; lower smooths it, leaving larger edges as they are.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Vibrance</th><td><Param path="adjust.vibrance.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.vibrance.max" /></td><td>Higher strengthens muted colors more than strong ones; lower mutes them.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Saturation</th><td><Param path="adjust.saturation.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.saturation.max" /></td><td>Higher strengthens every color alike; lower takes color out, to gray at the far left.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" colspan="3">Sharpening</th></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Amount</th><td><Param path="adjust.sharpening.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.sharpening.max" /></td><td>Higher sharpens edges more; a stacked result is already sharp, so a little goes a long way.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Radius</th><td><Param path="adjust.sharpen_radius.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.sharpen_radius.max" /> px, from <Param path="adjust.sharpen_radius.default" /></td><td>Higher sharpens a wider band around each edge; lower keeps it to the finest detail.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Detail</th><td><Param path="adjust.sharpen_detail.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.sharpen_detail.max" />, from <Param path="adjust.sharpen_detail.default" /></td><td>Higher sharpens the finest detail more; lower holds back halos along large edges.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Masking</th><td><Param path="adjust.sharpen_masking.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.sharpen_masking.max" /></td><td>Higher sharpens only the stronger edges, leaving smooth areas such as a plain background alone.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" colspan="3">Vignette</th></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Amount</th><td><Param path="adjust.vignette.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.vignette.max" /></td><td>Lower darkens the edges of the picture; higher lightens them.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Midpoint</th><td><Param path="adjust.vignette_midpoint.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.vignette_midpoint.max" />, from <Param path="adjust.vignette_midpoint.default" /></td><td>Lower starts the vignette nearer the center; higher keeps it to the corners.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A RAW or DNG stack's Temperature reads in kelvins, as the camera recorded it; its track is even up to
        <Param path="adjust.kelvin_center" /> K, the middle, and stretches out above it. A stack of JPEG, PNG or
        TIFF frames has no known temperature, so its Temperature and Tint run from
        <Param path="adjust.temperature_relative.min" /> to <Param path="adjust.temperature_relative.max" />,
        warmer or cooler than the frames. Sharpening's Radius, Detail and Masking change nothing while its Amount
        is 0, and the Vignette's Midpoint nothing while its Amount is 0. The vignette is centered on the Crop.
      </p>
      <p>
        Drag a slider, or pick one with ↑ and ↓ and move it a step with ← and →, ten with Shift. Click a
        slider's value to type one, then press Return, or Esc to keep the old value. Double-click a slider's
        name to set it back where it started; <em>Reset All</em>, at the bottom of the inspector, sets every
        slider back and leaves the Crop.
      </p>
      <p>
        While you drag, the picture follows at the size shown. Zoomed out, Texture and Sharpening wait until
        you let go, since a picture shrunk to fit can't show a filter a few pixels wide; from 50% zoom and
        closer they follow the drag too.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="white-balance">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Setting the white balance from the picture</h2>
      <p>
        Click the eyedropper on the White Balance heading, or press <em>W</em>, then click something that should
        be neutral gray or white. Temperature and Tint are set so that spot has no color cast. The spot is read
        from the render before any adjustment, so the other sliders don't change what it finds. A spot that is
        blown out or too dark is refused with a notice saying why. The eyedropper is for one
        click; Esc puts it away without one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="crop">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Crop, turn and flip</h2>
      <p>
        Click <em>Crop</em> in the inspector or press <em>C</em>. The whole picture shows, with the Crop
        marked. Drag a corner or an edge to resize it, or inside it to move it. The aspect choices are
        Original, Free, 1:1, 4:5, 3:2 and 16:9; <em>X</em> swaps the Crop's width and height. <em>Turn
        Left</em> and <em>Turn Right</em> (⌘[ and ⌘]) turn the picture a quarter at a time, and <em>Flip
        Horizontal</em> and <em>Flip Vertical</em> mirror it. <em>Reset Crop</em> takes off the Crop, the turn
        and the flip.
      </p>
      <p>
        Return or <em>Done</em> keeps the Crop; Esc puts it back as it was when you opened the tool. Outside the
        Crop tool, the result shows cropped, turned and flipped, and the Library's thumbnail follows once you
        leave Adjust. Cropping keeps the pixels: the Crop can always be widened again. It is Adjust's Crop, not
        stacking's <a href="/docs/alignment/#settings">Crop to the area every frame covers</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The turn and flip show in every view of the render: source frames, the depth map, comparisons and
        Retouch, so a click on any of them still lands on the right spot. Only the result is cropped; the
        others show the whole picture.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="before">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Before and after</h2>
      <p>
        Hold <em>\</em>, or hold <em>Before</em> at the bottom of the inspector, to see the render without its
        adjustments, with its retouching. Let go to see them again.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="copy">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Copy and Paste Adjustments</h2>
      <p>
        Edit ▸ Copy Adjustments (⇧⌘C) takes the sliders of the render shown, and Edit ▸ Paste Adjustments
        (⇧⌘V) sets another render's sliders to them, in Adjust or not. The Crop, turn and flip stay with each
        render, since one picture's framing rarely fits another.
      </p>
      <p>
        A new render of a stack starts with the sliders of the render it was made from, and with its Crop too
        when the two have the same framing, so a restack keeps your look. A stack with nothing to start from, and
        every render a Batch makes, starts with none.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="undo">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Undo and what is saved</h2>
      <p>
        Every change is one step to undo: a drag, a typed value, the eyedropper, a Crop, Reset All or a paste.
        ⌘Z undoes it and ⇧⌘Z redoes it (Ctrl+Y also redoes on Windows and Linux), back through the changes made
        to that render since Deptheos started, after leaving Adjust and coming back too.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each change is saved with the render as you make it, and is there when you open the render again, after
        a restart too. Adjust never changes the render's pixels, or your frames.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="export">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What exports keep</h2>
      <p>
        A TIFF, PNG or JPEG of an adjusted render is the picture Adjust shows, with every slider and the Crop,
        turn and flip, and its retouching. A RAW render can be saved in sRGB, Display P3, Adobe RGB or ProPhoto
        RGB (ProPhoto at 16 bits only), and 16 bits per channel is offered for any adjusted render, even from 8-bit
        frames. A render of JPEG, PNG or TIFF frames keeps their color space, unless its profile is one that
        can't take adjustments, which is saved as sRGB, and the sheet says so.
        <a href="/docs/export/#color">Exporting</a> has the details.
      </p>
      <p>
        A DNG keeps every pixel as it was stacked. Of the adjustments it carries only the white balance and the
        Crop, turn and flip, written as settings your RAW editor reads and can change; the other sliders don't
        reach it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Retouch shows the render without its adjustments, so you paint on the picture as it was stacked;
        leaving Retouch draws the adjustments over the new paint. Comparisons show each render with its own
        adjustments. A grow sequence takes the final render's sliders, but not its Crop, so every frame keeps
        the stack's size. Aligned frames are exported as stacked, and a turntable sequence is made as its Batch
        runs, before its renders have any adjustments.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="keys">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Keys</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="choose">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>A</kbd></th><td>Adjust, or Done Adjusting.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Esc</kbd></th><td>Leave Adjust; with the Crop tool or the eyedropper open, close that first.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>↑</kbd>, <kbd>↓</kbd></th><td>The previous or next slider.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>←</kbd>, <kbd>→</kbd></th><td>Move the slider a step; with <kbd>⇧</kbd>, ten.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>\</kbd> (held)</th><td>See Before.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>W</kbd></th><td>The white balance eyedropper.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>C</kbd></th><td>Crop, turn and flip.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>⌘[</kbd>, <kbd>⌘]</kbd></th><td>Turn left or right.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>X</kbd></th><td>Swap the Crop's width and height.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>↩</kbd></th><td>Keep the Crop.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>⇧⌘C</kbd>, <kbd>⇧⌘V</kbd></th><td>Copy Adjustments, Paste Adjustments.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>⌘Z</kbd>, <kbd>⇧⌘Z</kbd></th><td>Undo, Redo a change.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        From the command line, <a href="/docs/cli/#redraw"><code>deptheos redraw</code></a> draws a saved render
        with given adjustments, without opening the app's Library.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
